# Target C-II — near-30-degree convex-route penetration matching

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proved proof-only strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** current low-`t` full-core Type-II endpoint  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The `15`-degree window is useful because it gives an exact local phase-gate normal
form. It is not optimal for producing a large physical penetration matching.

After the merged convex-route sharpening, the straight-occurrence loss is only

\[
{d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}.
\]

We can therefore move the angular window very close to `30` degrees. The only reason
not to take `beta=pi/6` exactly is the matching argument: at the opposite endpoint we
need three incoming penetration rays to be impossible, and we need a penetration edge
to be quantitatively separated from a route edge at another straight vertex.

The current detour bound gives a uniform nearby-normal spread below `0.0028841`
radians. Choosing

\[
\boxed{\beta_*={\pi\over6}-0.003}
\]

leaves a strict finite margin for both requirements.

The result is

\[
\boxed{
M_*\ge0.6279410342628509\ldots D-o(D)
}

pairwise vertex-disjoint signed penetration contacts. Moreover at least

\[
\boxed{
0.1698248997679897\ldots D-o(D)
}

of a matching can be required to lie on non-singleton strict-rhombus trains. If the
outer tail is also required to be simple-outer, the train-supported matching floor is
still

\[
\boxed{
0.1468566298124728\ldots D-o(D).
}

This is strictly stronger than the `15`-degree matching for deformation/train work,
while the `15`-degree theorem remains stronger for local phase classification.

---

## 1. Convex straight-occurrence count at `beta_*`

Put

\[
\varepsilon:=0.003,
\qquad
\beta_*={\pi\over6}-\varepsilon,
\tag{N30.1}
\]

and

\[
c_*:=1-\cos\beta_*.
\]

The merged convex-route theorem gives

\[
S_*
\ge
x_B-{d_{out}\over c_*}.
\tag{N30.2}
\]

With the usual normalized variables

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},
\]

this becomes

\[
\liminf {S_*\over D}
\ge
\pi-t-\kappa-
\left({1\over c_*}-1\right)\delta.
\tag{N30.3}
\]

At `beta_*`,

\[
{1\over c_*}-1
=6.548394897186289\ldots>4.
\]

Thus the low-`t` constraint

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R(t)
\]

again has its worst endpoint at `delta=R(t)/2`, `kappa=0`. At `t=t_42`,

\[
\boxed{
{S_*\over D}
\ge1.3018186084367357059\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{N30.4}

This is an occurrence count.

---

## 2. Distinct physical straight tails

Let `V_out` be the number of distinct physical outer-face vertices. The merged
near-simple theorem gives the full repetition-surplus bound

\[
{b-V_{out}\over D}
\le0.0459365399110338116\ldots+o(1).
\tag{N30.5}
\]

If a physical vertex supports `m_x` outer occurrences, then any straight occurrences
at that vertex can exceed one by at most `m_x-1`. Therefore

\[
S_*-V_*
\le
b-V_{out},
\tag{N30.6}
\]

where `V_*` is the number of distinct physical vertices supporting at least one
`beta_*`-straight occurrence. Hence

\[
\boxed{
{V_*\over D}
\ge1.2558820685257018943\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{N30.7}

Because `beta_*<pi/6`, the degree-collapse theorem applies at every chosen occurrence:
each tail is unloaded and has contact degree exactly three or four.

For each physical tail in `V_*`, choose one of its straight occurrences and then
choose one extra contact edge at that occurrence. Denote the resulting set by `E_*`.

---

## 3. Signed inward margin

At every chosen occurrence the signed straight-event theorem gives

\[
(w-x)\cdot n_i
>
\sin(\pi/3-\beta_*)
=\sin(\pi/6+\varepsilon).
\tag{N30.8}
\]

Numerically,

\[
\boxed{
(w-x)\cdot n_i>0.5025958223159282\ldots.
}
\tag{N30.9}

A route edge at a `beta_*`-straight occurrence has absolute normal component less than

\[
\sin\beta_*
=0.4973996776874467\ldots.
\tag{N30.10}
\]

The finite gap between (N30.9) and (N30.10) is

\[
\boxed{0.0051961446284815\ldots.}
\tag{N30.11}
\]

---

## 4. Nearby outer tails have almost identical hull normals

The signed-penetration matching audit proved the general bounded-distance estimate.
If two outer-route occurrences are at Euclidean distance `O(1)` and their associated
hull-side inward normals make angle `theta`, then

\[
\sin{\theta\over2}
\le {\delta\over2}+o(1).
\tag{N30.12}
\]

At the current endpoint `delta<=0.002883975+o(1)`, so

\[
\boxed{
\theta
\le
2\arcsin(0.0014419875)+o(1)
=0.0028839759994559\ldots+o(1).
}
\tag{N30.13}

Call the displayed finite constant `theta_0`. Our choice

\[
\varepsilon=0.003>\theta_0
\tag{N30.14}
\]

has a positive margin.

---

## 5. The chosen physical edges are distinct

Suppose one physical edge `xy` were chosen as an extra contact at straight occurrences
at both endpoints. Let `e=y-x`. Equation (N30.8) at the two endpoints gives

\[
e\cdot n_i>\sin(\pi/6+\varepsilon),
\]

and

\[
e\cdot n_j<-\sin(\pi/6+\varepsilon).
\]

Thus the two hull normals are separated by a fixed angle strictly larger than

\[
\pi-2\left({\pi\over3}-\varepsilon\right)
={\pi\over3}+2\varepsilon.
\]

The merged signed-injectivity geometry then forces the corresponding hull-side
midpoints to be a positive fraction of `D` apart, while the two outer endpoints are
unit distance apart. The route-midpoint estimate would require `Theta(D)` total outer
detour, contradicting `d_out<=0.002883975D+o(D)`.

Hence the one-per-tail selection is globally edge-injective:

\[
\boxed{|E_*|=V_*.}
\tag{N30.15}

---

## 6. At most two chosen penetrations share one opposite endpoint

Suppose distinct chosen contacts

\[
x_1w,\ldots,x_rw
\]

share the same opposite endpoint `w`. Their outer tails satisfy

\[
|x_a-x_b|\le2,
\]

so their hull normals have pairwise angular spread at most `theta_0+o(1)` by
(N30.13).

A selected direction `e_a=w-x_a` lies within angle

\[
\arccos\sin(\pi/6+\varepsilon)
={\pi\over3}-\varepsilon
\]

of its local normal. Therefore all incoming directions lie in an angular interval of
width at most

\[
2\left({\pi\over3}-\varepsilon+\theta_0\right)+o(1).
\tag{N30.16}
\]

By (N30.14), this is strictly less than `2pi/3` for all sufficiently large `D`.

At `w`, triangle-freeness forces the contact rays toward distinct `x_a` to have
pairwise angular separation strictly greater than `pi/3`. Three such rays cannot fit
in an interval shorter than `2pi/3`. Hence

\[
\boxed{r\le2.}
\tag{N30.17}

---

## 7. The opposite endpoint is not another chosen straight tail

Let `xw` be chosen at tail `x` and suppose `w` supports a chosen `beta_*`-straight
occurrence. Since `|x-w|=1`, their hull normals differ by at most `theta_0+o(1)`.

If `xw` is extra at `w`, the signed inequalities point the same unit edge inward at
both endpoints with opposite orientations, which is incompatible with nearly equal
normals.

If `xw` is a route edge at `w`, then by (N30.10)

\[
|e\cdot n_w|<\sin\beta_*.
\]

Changing the unit normal by angle at most `theta_0+o(1)` changes this dot product by at
most `theta_0+o(1)`. Therefore

\[
e\cdot n_x
<
\sin\beta_*+\theta_0+o(1).
\]

The verifier checks the strict finite inequality

\[
\sin\beta_*+\theta_0
<
\sin(\pi/6+\varepsilon),
\tag{N30.18}
\]

contradicting (N30.8).

Thus the chosen tail set and opposite-endpoint set are disjoint.

---

## 8. Physical matching floor

The graph with left side the distinct tails `V_*`, right side their opposite endpoints,
and edge set `E_*` is bipartite. Every left vertex has degree one by construction, and
every right vertex has degree at most two by (N30.17). Therefore its edges can be
covered by a matching of size at least half:

\[
\nu(E_*)\ge{|E_*|\over2}={V_*\over2}.
\]

Using (N30.7),

\[
\boxed{
{M_*\over D}
\ge0.6279410342628509471\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{N30.19}

These contacts are pairwise vertex-disjoint in the physical source graph and each has
signed inward component greater than `0.5025958`.

---

## 9. Simple-outer and train-supported subfamilies

A straight physical vertex is simple-outer if it has no other outer-face occurrence.
To lower-bound the simple-outer straight population from an occurrence count, one pays
the repetition surplus twice: once to deduplicate straight occurrences and once to
remove repeated physical vertices. Hence

\[
V_{simp,*}
\ge
S_*-2(b-V_{out}).
\tag{N30.20}
\]

At the current endpoint,

\[
\boxed{
{V_{simp,*}\over D}
\ge1.2099455286146680827\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{N30.21}

Choose one extra contact per such simple tail. The same injectivity and endpoint
arguments apply.

Use the conservative independently audited singleton stopped-train budget

\[
{S_{train}\over D}
\le0.9162322689897223521\ldots+o(1).
\tag{N30.22}

At most that many selected physical edges can avoid all strict rhombi. Thus

\[
\boxed{
{E_{R,*}\over D}
\ge
1.2558820685257018943
-0.9162322689897223521
-o(1)
}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
{E_{R,*}\over D}
\ge0.3396497995359795422\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{N30.23}

The matching argument survives restriction to this subfamily:

\[
\boxed{
{M_{R,*}\over D}
\ge0.1698248997679897711\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{N30.24}

For simple-outer tails,

\[
\boxed{
{E_{R,simp,*}\over D}
\ge0.2937132596249457306\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{N30.25}

and hence

\[
\boxed{
{M_{R,simp,*}\over D}
\ge0.1468566298124728653\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{N30.26}

---

## 10. Two-window frontier

The strongest current use of the outer-route geometry is now a two-window argument.

### `beta=pi/12`

Use this narrower window for the exact local phase classification:

- degree three or four;
- positive local turn `<pi/6`;
- cubic mixed/double-obtuse dichotomy;
- quartic acute-fan identity;
- retained phase-gate accounting.

The convex-route update gives at least `1.1930133014D-o(D)` simple-outer vertices in
this rigid class.

### `beta=pi/6-0.003`

Use this wider window for deformation and train support:

- `>=1.2558820685D-o(D)` distinct straight physical tails;
- `>=0.6279410342D-o(D)` vertex-disjoint signed penetration matching;
- `>=0.1698248997D-o(D)` train-supported matching;
- `>=0.1468566298D-o(D)` simple-outer train-supported matching.

The remaining obstruction is no longer endpoint reuse. A macroscopic rhombic strip can
still carry many of these pairwise vertex-disjoint penetration edges along one train.
The next theorem must therefore control strip occupancy by boundary turning/phase,
produce a source-level strip competitor, or force too many independent diameter
braces.

**C-II remains open.**
